  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech transformation of a negative command: 'The swimming instructor warned the children, \"Do not dive into the shallow end.\"'", o: ["The swimming instructor warned the children not to dive into the shallow end.","The swimming instructor told the children not dive into the shallow end.","The swimming instructor said the children not to dive into the shallow end.","The swimming instructor ordered that do not dive into the shallow end."], a: 0, e: "Negative imperatives convert using 'not to' preceded by a warning reporting verb." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts an exclamatory sentence into indirect speech: 'She exclaimed, \"What a wonderful melody this is!\"'", o: ["She exclaimed with delight that that was a wonderful melody.","She exclaimed that what a wonderful melody that was.","She asked whether that was a wonderful melody.","She said that what a wonderful melody this is."], a: 0, e: "Exclamations drop exclamation marks, use emotive reporting verbs ('exclaimed with delight'), shift demonstratives ('this' -> 'that'), and backshift tenses ('is' -> 'was')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech form featuring modal backshifting: 'The carpenter said, \"I can carve a wooden sculpture by tomorrow.\"'", o: ["The carpenter said that he could carve a wooden sculpture by the next day.","The carpenter said that he can carve a wooden sculpture by tomorrow.","The carpenter said that he could carve a wooden sculpture by tomorrow.","The carpenter said that he would carve a wooden sculpture by the next day."], a: 0, e: "Modal 'can' backshifts to 'could', and 'tomorrow' changes to 'the next day'." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech transformation when reporting a request: 'The traveler asked, \"Could you show me on the map where the station is?\"'", o: ["The traveler requested me to show him/her on the map where the station was.","The traveler asked whether I could please show him/her on the map where the station was.","Both options A and B are grammatically acceptable indirect forms.","The traveler asked that could I show him/her on the map where the station is."], a: 2, e: "Polite requests can be reported via infinitive requests ('requested someone to') or modal questions ('asked whether...'), with internal clause backshifting ('is' -> 'was')." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a sentence with 'must' of strict regulation into indirect speech: 'The park ranger said, \"Campers must extinguish their campfires before sleeping.\"'", o: ["The park ranger said that campers had to extinguish their campfires before sleeping. (or '...must extinguish...')","The park ranger said that campers must had extinguished their campfires before sleeping.","The park ranger said that campers would have to extinguish their campfires before sleeping.","Both options A and C are accepted depending on context."], a: 3, e: "'Must' expressing park regulations or safety rules backshifts to 'had to' or 'would have to', or may remain 'must'." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech form when the reporting verb is in the present tense: 'The pilot announces, \"We are flying at an altitude of 35,000 feet right now.\"'", o: ["The pilot announces that they are flying at an altitude of 35,000 feet right now.","The pilot announces that they were flying at an altitude of 35,000 feet right then.","The pilot announces that they have been flying at an altitude of 35,000 feet right now.","The pilot announces that they had been flying at an altitude of 35,000 feet right then."], a: 0, e: "When the reporting verb ('announces') is in the present tense, no tense backshift or adverbial shift occurs." },
  { q: "Identify the correct adverbial shift in indirect speech: 'He said, \"I bought these fresh oranges from the orchard yesterday.\"'", o: ["He said that he had bought those fresh oranges from the orchard the previous day.","He said that he bought these fresh oranges from the orchard yesterday.","He said that he has bought those fresh oranges from the orchard yesterday.","He said that he had bought these fresh oranges from the orchard the previous day."], a: 0, e: "'These' changes to 'those', 'yesterday' changes to 'the previous day', and simple past backshifts to past perfect ('had bought')." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech sentence containing 'today' into indirect speech: 'She said, \"We are planting rose saplings in the garden today.\"'", o: ["She said that they were planting rose saplings in the garden that day.","She said that they are planting rose saplings in the garden today.","She said that they were planting rose saplings in the garden today.","She said that they had planted rose saplings in the garden that day."], a: 0, e: "'Today' changes to 'that day', and present continuous backshifts to past continuous ('were planting')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'She said to her neighbor, \"Thank you for watering my plants while I was on vacation.\"'", o: ["She thanked her neighbor for watering her plants while she was on vacation.","She told her neighbor that thank you for watering my plants while I was on vacation.","She said to her neighbor that she thanked her for watering my plants.","She thanked for watering her plants."], a: 0, e: "Expressions of gratitude convert directly using the reporting verb 'thanked [object] for [gerund]' alongside pronoun adjustments ('my' -> 'her', 'I' -> 'she')." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form of a morning greeting: 'He said, \"Good morning, neighbor. How is your garden growing?\"'", o: ["He greeted his neighbor and asked how his garden was growing.","He said good morning to his neighbor and how is your garden growing.","He greeted his neighbor that how your garden was growing.","He wished his neighbor a good morning and asked how was your garden growing."], a: 0, e: "Combined greetings and questions convert using appropriate reporting verbs ('greeted' and 'asked') with pronoun shifts ('your' -> 'his') and tense backshifts ('is growing' -> 'was growing')." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech question starting with 'Shall we...' (proposal): 'He asked, \"Shall we visit the historical museum this afternoon?\"'", o: ["He proposed visiting (or suggested that they visit) the historical museum that afternoon.","He asked whether they should visit the historical museum that afternoon.","Both options A and B are grammatically correct indirect transformations.","He asked if shall they visit the historical museum this afternoon."], a: 2, e: "Proposals introduced by 'Shall we...' can be reported using 'proposed + gerund', 'suggested that... (should)', or as a modal question, with time shift ('this afternoon' -> 'that afternoon')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'The headmaster said, \"Let us organize an annual sports meet next month.\"'", o: ["The headmaster suggested that they organize (or should organize) an annual sports meet the following month.","The headmaster told to let them organize an annual sports meet next month.","The headmaster ordered that let us organize an annual sports meet the following month.","The headmaster proposed whether they organize an annual sports meet next month."], a: 0, e: "'Let us' suggestions convert using reporting verbs like 'suggested' followed by 'that they... (should)', with time shifts ('next month' -> 'the following month')." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form for past continuous statements: 'She said, \"I was knitting a woolen scarf while listening to the radio.\"'", o: ["She said that she had been knitting a woolen scarf while listening to the radio.","She said that she was knitting a woolen scarf while listening to the radio.","She said that she had been knitting a woolen scarf while had been listening to the radio.","She said that she has been knitting a woolen scarf while listening to the radio."], a: 0, e: "Past continuous ('was knitting') backshifts to past perfect continuous ('had been knitting'), while simultaneous participial phrases ('while listening') remain unchanged." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech statement containing 'this season' into indirect speech: 'The farmer said, \"We harvested a bumper crop of apples this season.\"'", o: ["The farmer said that they had harvested a bumper crop of apples that season.","The farmer said that they harvested a bumper crop of apples this season.","The farmer said that they have harvested a bumper crop of apples that season.","The farmer said that they would harvest a bumper crop of apples this season."], a: 0, e: "'This season' changes to 'that season', pronouns adjust ('we' -> 'they'), and simple past backshifts to past perfect ('had harvested')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'The optician asked, \"Have you noticed any strain in your eyes lately?\"'", o: ["The optician asked whether I had noticed any strain in my eyes lately.","The optician asked if have you noticed any strain in your eyes lately.","The optician asked whether did I notice any strain in my eyes lately.","The optician asked that I had noticed any strain in my eyes lately."], a: 0, e: "Present perfect yes/no question converts using 'whether' + past perfect ('had noticed'), with pronoun adjustments ('your' -> 'my')." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form of a conditional sentence: 'The meteorologist said, \"If the wind picks up, the storm will intensify.\"'", o: ["The meteorologist said that if the wind picked up, the storm would intensify.","The meteorologist said that if the wind picks up, the storm will intensify.","The meteorologist said that if the wind had picked up, the storm would have intensified.","The meteorologist said that if the wind would pick up, the storm would intensify."], a: 0, e: "First conditional clauses backshift tenses in indirect speech (present simple 'picks up' -> past simple 'picked up'; 'will' -> 'would')." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech greeting and question: 'He said, \"Welcome! How was your journey across the mountains?\"'", o: ["He welcomed me and asked how my journey across the mountains had been.","He said welcome and how was your journey across the mountains.","He welcomed me that how my journey across the mountains was.","He told welcome and asked how had been your journey."], a: 0, e: "Combined greetings ('Welcome!') and past simple questions convert using reporting verbs ('welcomed' and 'asked') with pronoun shifts ('your' -> 'my') and tense backshifts ('was' -> 'had been')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'The theater director announced, \"We are staging a classic Shakespearean play next winter.\"'", o: ["The theater director announced that they were staging a classic Shakespearean play the following winter.","The theater director announced that they are staging a classic Shakespearean play next winter.","The theater director announced that they will stage a classic Shakespearean play the following winter.","The theater director announced that they had been staging a classic Shakespearean play next winter."], a: 0, e: "Present continuous backshifts to past continuous ('were staging'), and 'next winter' changes to 'the following winter'." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form when reporting past modal speculation: 'She said, \"The antique vase might have been damaged during transport.\"'", o: ["She said that the antique vase might have been damaged during transport.","She said that the antique vase might had been damaged during transport.","She said that the antique vase may have been damaged during transport.","She said that the antique vase would have been damaged during transport."], a: 0, e: "Past modal passive perfects ('might have been damaged') remain unchanged in indirect speech." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech statement containing 'right now' into indirect speech: 'The baker said, \"Fresh croissants are coming out of the oven right now.\"'", o: ["The baker said that fresh croissants were coming out of the oven right then.","The baker said that fresh croissants are coming out of the oven right now.","The baker said that fresh croissants were coming out of the oven right now.","The baker said that fresh croissants had been coming out of the oven right then."], a: 0, e: "'Right now' changes to 'right then', and present continuous ('are coming') backshifts to past continuous ('were coming')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'The driving instructor asked the student, \"Why were you drifting into the adjacent lane?\"'", o: ["The driving instructor asked the student why he had been drifting into the adjacent lane.","The driving instructor asked the student why was he drifting into the adjacent lane.","The driving instructor asked the student why had he been drifting into the adjacent lane.","The driving instructor asked the student why he was drifting into the adjacent lane."], a: 0, e: "Past continuous Wh- question backshifts to past perfect continuous ('had been drifting') with statement word order." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form for a counterfactual wish: 'She said, \"I wish I had learned to play the piano during childhood.\"'", o: ["She said that she wished she had learned to play the piano during childhood.","She said that she wishes she had learned to play the piano during childhood.","She said that she had wished she had learned to play the piano during childhood.","She said that if only she had learned to play the piano during childhood."], a: 0, e: "Past counterfactual wishes ('wished' + past perfect 'had learned') are reported with appropriate pronoun adjustments while retaining their tense structure." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech statement with 'must' indicating logical certainty into indirect speech: 'He said, \"The locked front door must mean the shop is closed for the holiday.\"'", o: ["He said that the locked front door must mean the shop was closed for the holiday.","He said that the locked front door had to mean the shop is closed for the holiday.","He said that the locked front door would mean the shop was closed for the holiday.","He said that the locked front door must have meant the shop was closed for the holiday."], a: 0, e: "'Must' expressing logical deduction does not backshift, while the subordinate present tense verb ('is') backshifts to past tense ('was')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'The experienced mountaineer warned us, \"Beware of sudden rockfalls on the steep ridge.\"'", o: ["The experienced mountaineer warned us to beware of sudden rockfalls on the steep ridge.","The experienced mountaineer told us that beware of sudden rockfalls on the steep ridge.","The experienced mountaineer said us to beware of sudden rockfalls on the steep ridge.","The experienced mountaineer warned us that we are beware of sudden rockfalls."], a: 0, e: "Warning imperatives convert using 'warned [object] to [infinitive]'." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form when reporting a polite request with 'would you mind': 'He asked, \"Would you mind shifting your luggage slightly?\"'", o: ["He asked whether I would mind shifting my luggage slightly. (or 'He asked me to shift...')","He asked that would I mind shifting my luggage slightly.","He asked if did I mind shifting my luggage slightly.","He asked me shifting my luggage slightly."], a: 0, e: "Polite requests introduced by 'Would you mind...' convert using 'asked whether...' or infinitive request forms, with pronoun adjustments ('your' -> 'my')." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech statement featuring 'last autumn' into indirect speech: 'She said, \"We traveled through Europe last autumn.\"'", o: ["She said that they had traveled through Europe the previous autumn.","She said that they traveled through Europe last autumn.","She said that they have traveled through Europe the previous autumn.","She said that they would travel through Europe the previous autumn."], a: 0, e: "'Last autumn' changes to 'the previous autumn', and simple past backshifts to past perfect ('had traveled')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'The grandfather said to his grandson, \"Congratulations on graduating with honors!\"'", o: ["The grandfather congratulated his grandson on graduating with honors.","The grandfather told his grandson that congratulations on graduating with your honors.","The grandfather said his grandson congratulations on graduating with honors.","The grandfather congratulated that his grandson graduated with honors."], a: 0, e: "Expressions of congratulations convert directly using the reporting verb 'congratulated [object] on [gerund]'." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form of a second conditional sentence: 'He said, \"If I owned a coastal cottage, I would watch the sunset every evening.\"'", o: ["He said that if he owned a coastal cottage, he would watch the sunset every evening.","He said that if he had owned a coastal cottage, he would have watched the sunset every evening.","He said that if he owns a coastal cottage, he will watch the sunset every evening.","He said that if he would own a coastal cottage, he would watch the sunset every evening."], a: 0, e: "Second conditional sentences in indirect speech generally do not backshift their tenses because they already represent counterfactual/hypothetical states." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech exclamation starting with 'What an...' into indirect speech: 'She exclaimed, \"What an extraordinary discovery this is!\"'", o: ["She exclaimed with excitement that that was an extraordinary discovery.","She exclaimed that what an extraordinary discovery that was.","She asked whether that was an extraordinary discovery.","She said that what an extraordinary discovery this is."], a: 0, e: "Exclamations with 'What an...' convert by dropping 'What an', using emotive reporting verbs ('exclaimed with excitement'), shifting demonstratives ('this' -> 'that'), and backshifting tenses ('is' -> 'was')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'The captain whispered, \"Let us anchor the boat near the secluded cove.\"'", o: ["The captain suggested anchoring (or that they should anchor) the boat near the secluded cove.","The captain told to let anchor the boat near the secluded cove.","The captain ordered that let us anchor the boat near the secluded cove.","The captain proposed whether anchoring the boat near the secluded cove."], a: 0, e: "'Let us' suggestions convert using 'suggested + gerund' or 'suggested that... should'." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form when reporting a third conditional sentence: 'She said, \"If we had packed extra woolens, we would not have felt cold in the mountains.\"'", o: ["She said that if they had packed extra woolens, they would not have felt cold in the mountains.","She said that if they packed extra woolens, they would not feel cold in the mountains.","She said that if they have packed extra woolens, they would not have felt cold in the mountains.","She said that if they would have packed extra woolens, they had felt cold in the mountains."], a: 0, e: "Third conditional sentences remain unchanged in indirect speech because past perfect and past modal perfects cannot backshift further." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech statement containing 'up to now' into indirect speech: 'He said, \"I have traveled across twelve countries up to now.\"'", o: ["He said that he had traveled across twelve countries up to then.","He said that he has traveled across twelve countries up to now.","He said that he traveled across twelve countries up to now.","He said that he would travel across twelve countries up to then."], a: 0, e: "Present perfect ('have traveled') backshifts to past perfect ('had traveled'), and 'up to now' changes to 'up to then'." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'The librarian asked, \"Whose notebook was left on the reading desk?\"'", o: ["The librarian asked whose notebook had been left on the reading desk.","The librarian asked whose notebook was left on the reading desk.","The librarian asked whose notebook is left on the reading desk.","The librarian asked whose notebook left on the reading desk was."], a: 0, e: "Whose-questions retain the interrogative word, use statement word order, and simple past passive ('was left') frequently backshifts to past perfect passive ('had been left') or remains past passive depending on temporal distance." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form when reporting a universal botanical fact: 'The botanist said, \"Sunflowers turn their blossoms toward the Sun.\"'", o: ["The botanist said that sunflowers turn their blossoms toward the Sun.","The botanist said that sunflowers turned their blossoms toward the Sun.","The botanist said that sunflowers had turned their blossoms toward the Sun.","The botanist said that sunflowers are turning their blossoms toward the Sun."], a: 0, e: "Natural botanical facts and biological traits remain in the simple present tense in indirect speech." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct speech statement with an introductory vocative: 'She said, \"Arjun, please pass me the salt shaker.\"'", o: ["She asked Arjun to pass her the salt shaker.","She told that Arjun please pass me the salt shaker.","She said Arjun to pass her the salt shaker.","She asked to Arjun that he pass her the salt shaker."], a: 0, e: "The vocative name in direct address ('Arjun') becomes the object of the reporting verb in indirect speech ('She asked Arjun to...'), with pronoun adjustments ('me' -> 'her')." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'He muttered, \"Confound this sudden thunderstorm during our hike!\"'", o: ["He expressed frustration at the sudden thunderstorm during their hike. (or 'He cursed...')","He said that he confounded the sudden thunderstorm during our hike.","He exclaimed with anger that that sudden thunderstorm during their hike was bad.","He wished that the sudden thunderstorm confounded."], a: 0, e: "Strong emotional outbursts convert using precise reporting verbs like 'expressed frustration' or 'cursed', with pronoun adjustments ('our' -> 'their')." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form of a multi-clause statement: 'She said, \"I baked fresh bread, and I brewed a pot of herbal tea.\"'", o: ["She said that she had baked fresh bread and (that) she had brewed a pot of herbal tea.","She said that she baked fresh bread, and she brewed a pot of herbal tea.","She said that she has baked fresh bread and she has brewed a pot of herbal tea.","She said that she would bake fresh bread and she brewed a pot of herbal tea."], a: 0, e: "In multi-clause statements joined by coordinating conjunctions, both simple past clauses undergo standard backshifting to past perfect ('had baked' and 'had brewed')." },
  { q: "Which option correctly converts a direct alternative question: 'The flight attendant asked, \"Would you prefer a window seat or an aisle seat?\"'", o: ["The flight attendant asked whether I would prefer a window seat or an aisle seat.","The flight attendant asked that I would prefer a window seat or an aisle seat.","The flight attendant asked if would I prefer a window seat or an aisle seat.","The flight attendant asked whether or not preferring a window seat."], a: 0, e: "Alternative questions convert using 'whether' followed by statement word order and modal retention/adjustment." },
  { q: "Choose the correct indirect speech transformation of: 'The museum curator instructed the visitors, \"Do not touch the delicate marble statues.\"'", o: ["The museum curator instructed the visitors not to touch the delicate marble statues.","The museum curator told the visitors that do not touch the delicate marble statues.","The museum curator said the visitors not touching the delicate marble statues.","The museum curator ordered that the visitors not touch the delicate marble statues."], a: 0, e: "Instructional prohibitions convert using 'instructed [object] + not to + infinitive'." },
  { q: "Identify the correct indirect speech form when reporting a past regret with 'should have': 'He said, \"I should have checked the weather forecast before setting sail.\"'", o: ["He said that he should have checked the weather forecast before setting sail.","He said that he should had checked the weather forecast before setting sail.","He said that he must have checked the weather forecast before setting sail.","He said that he would have checked the weather forecast before setting sail."], a: 0, e: "Past modal perfects ('should have checked') remain unchanged in indirect speech." }
